<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umberto I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1844–1900</span> · Also known as Umberto I of Italy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umberto I was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1878 to 1900, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>. His reign saw the Triple Alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the first colonial ventures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896. After troops fired on demonstrators in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1898, the anarchist Gaetano Bresci shot him dead at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a> on 29 July 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umberto-i.jpg" alt="Umberto I" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fratelli Vianelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fratelli_Vianelli_(Giuseppe_e_Luigi,_flor._1860-1890_ca)_-_VE_-_Umberto_I_di_Savoia_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
